This document is an elaboration on materials and principles taught over the course of several classes at _Klingenspiel_#index[Klingenspiel] in Vienna.
The focus of these lessons were on ways of actively using balance to prepare and initiate actions, especially voids.
To make that possible, however, a fencer must be able to control and manipulate their balance, both in the guard and in transitory and preparatory positions.
For many fencers, however, balance is something they try to retain (to 'keep one's balance'), not something they actively use.
To remedy that, the first section of this document contains drills and exercises aimed at developing an awareness of balance in various positions and movements.
It also contains descriptions of how I manipulate my balance to initiate various common pieces of footwork.
The second section combines these movements into simple solo- and partner drills.
The third and final section contains partner drills that gradually build from simple action/reaction to something resembling constrained sparring.

=== Fundamentals
The human body has two centres of balance.
One, the head, is gimballed, the other, the 'centre of gravity', is located somewhere around the navel; moving either causes the rest of the body to follow.
It can be helpful to think of the rapier as a third centre of balance.
It is not as potent as the other two, but especially a heavier rapier can significantly alter one's balance.
For the purposes of this chapter then, one can think of 'balance'#index[Balance] as the equilibrium between the two centres, the rapier, and whatever is counteracting gravity---usually one or both feet.
The distance and orientation of your feet, your 'base', is another important factor.
Standing on both feet, set roughly shoulder-width apart provides a stable platform, standing on the toes of a single foot does not.
As we shall see, however, stability is not always desirable, and neither is a broad, stable base.

The first step toward intentionally manipulating one's balance is to become sensitive to it.
Therefore, before working on any of the material below---preferably before reading any further---take your rapier and move as you usually would, observing your balance as you do so.
As you work through the rest of this chapter, endeavour to come back to this awareness; it is the basis for everything below.

===== Guard#index[Guard]
While this chapter is agnostic to the style of your guard, but there are a some things that I have found helpful: #footnote[For a thorough discussion of rapier guards, see @finding-the-guard, as well as @developing-the-guard.]
- Place the majority of your weight on the balls of your feet. This does not mean lifting your heels of the floor, however!
- While in your guard, slowly move your one or both of your centres of balance in a direction until you cross the point where you can no longer stay in place and have to catch yourself.
- Practice moving right up to that 'point of no return' and stay there.
- Change a small detail---the angle of your back foot, the distance between your feet ('North/South' and or 'East/West'), or the angle of your upper body---and observe how that changes any of the above.

When working through the rest of this chapter, periodically come back to the guard and see if anything needs altering.

===== Drop step#index[Drop step]<balance-and-voiding_drop-step>
The drop step is used across martial arts, but I became aware of it through the work of Rory Miller#index[Miller, Rory].
In essence, a drop step in an intentional free fall.
One can think of it as lifting one foot off the ground without first taking one's weight off of it, and the catching oneself before hitting the ground.
The human body is pretty good at keeping itself from landing face-first on the floor, and so it can help to focus on removing the leg swiftly, and let the catching take care of itself.
For a detailed description, see Rory Miller's#index[Miller, Rory] #link("https://chirontraining.blogspot.com/2011/10/drop-step.html")[blog post] on the topic.
#footnote[As Miller point out, it is also possible to drop straight down by 'lifting' both feet at the same time. This is not very idiomatic to Italian rapier fencing at all, but can nonetheless catch an opponent by surprise.]

===== Altered drop step#index[Drop step]<balance-and-voiding_altered-drop-step>

A limitation of the drop step is that it always results in a movement along the line between one's feet.
By shifting one's weight or leaning in the direction wants to 'fall', however, it is possible to alter that trajectory, at the cost of telegraphing one's intention.
When practising this, start by clearly separating the two stages of the movement: first shift weight and/or lean, then perform a drop step.
Experiment with different amounts of leaning and shifting, from barely noticeable to falling over---both ends of that spectrum can be very useful.

===== Lunge#index[Lunge]<balance-and-voiding_lunge>

What I have described as an altered drop step above, is almost identical to the footwork of a rapier lunge.
#footnote[Or at least the way I teach it. The proverbial cat is making itself scarce again.]
All that is missing, is to add the upper-body parts of the lunge (extending the arm, turning the torso, getting behind the hilt, etc.).
A few things to keep in mind:
- the order of movement is: extend arm--lean/shift--drop step--catch--recover or advance. Always extend the arm first!
- at no point during the lunge should one push off of the back foot.#footnote[This assumes that one leaves the back foot in place during the lunge, which I firmly believe is the correct thing to do.]

Once that begins to feel smooth, find the 'point of no return'---the point beyond which one has to commit to the drop step.
Then practice getting to that point as quickly as possible, without kipping over into a full lunge.
That point, and being able to explosively get to it, goes a long way towards feinting#index[Feint] convincingly (see also @cue_explosive-impetus).

===== Traverso Weapon Side#index[Traverso]<balance-and-voiding_traverso-ws>
A traverso, from the word for 'going across', is mechanically similar to a lunge, but instead of forwards, one moves diagonally or sidewards.
When 'traversing' to the weapon side, it is often easiest to go diagonally forward, as that is closest to the strong line.
One thing to note about a traverso to the weapon side is that it brings the blade off-line.
While this does create an opening---which can be addressed with the #link(<empty-hand-basics>)[empty hand]#index[Empty Hand]---it also allows one to attack from different angles.

===== Traverso Empty-hand side#index[Traverso]<balance-and-voiding_traverso-es>
For the same reason that a traverso to the weapon side tends to move diagonally forward, a traverso to the empty-hand side tends to move diagonally backward.
Contrary to a traverso to the weapon side, it is possible to leave the rapier on-line; one can think of leaving the blade in place and moving the body around it.

===== Passata Sotto#index[Passata Sotto]<balance-and-voiding_passata-sotto>
Shift weight back, then drop step
The passata sotto is quite an athletic movement and an excellent way to pull one's groin if one is not used to it.

